Goth. ''thar'' (for the expected *''thēr''), there, in that place; orig. a locative form (nearly like the dat. and instr. fem. sing. ''thǣre'') of the pronominal stem *''tha'', appearing in ''the'', ''that'', etc., also in ''then'', etc. Cf. ''here''<sup>1</sup>, ''where''; Skt. ''tarhi'', then, ''karhi'', when. In comp. ''there'' is the adverb in its literal use, or, in ''therein'', ''therefor'', etc., in a quasi-pronominal use, ''therein'' being 'in that (sc. place),' ''thereby'' being 'by that (sc. means),' etc. ''There'' is therefore explained by some as really the dat. fem. sing. of the AS. def. art., but such use of a fem. form (instead of the expected neuter), in such a way, is unexampled; and the explanation cannot apply to the similar elements ''here-'' and ''where-'' as used in composition.] '''I.''' ''adv.'' '''1.''' In or at a definite place other than that occupied by the speaker; in that place; at that point: used in reference to a place or point otherwise or already indicated or known: as, you will find him ''there'' (pointing to the particular place); if he is in Paris, I shall see him ''there''. {{smaller|It is often opposed to ''here'', ''there'' generally denoting the place more distant; but in some cases the words when used together are employed merely in contradistinction, without reference to nearness or distance.}}

{{smaller block|Stand thou ''there'', or sit here under my footstool.
{{right|Jas. ii. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|You have a house i' the country; keep you ''there'', sir.
{{right|''Fletcher'', Loyal Subject, i. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|All life is but a wandering to find home;
When we are gone, we're ''there''.
{{right|''Ford and Dekker'', Witch of Edmonton, iv. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|Of this the ''there'' born Emperour Adrian received his name.
{{right|''Sandys'', Travailes, p. 2.}}}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|Darkness ''there'' might well<br>Seem twilight here. {{right|''Milton'', P. L., vi. 11.}}}}

'''2.''' Into that place; to that place; thither: after verbs of motion or direction: as, how did that get ''there?'' I will go ''there'' to-morrow.

{{smaller block|My heart stands armed in mine ear,<br>And will not let a false sound enter ''there.'' {{right|''Shak.'', Venus and Adonis, l. 780.}}}}

{{smaller block|There was Lord Belfast, that by me past<br>And seemed to ask how should I go ''there?'' {{right|''Thackeray'', Mr. Molony's Account of the Ball.}}}}

'''3.''' At that point of progress; after going so far or proceeding to such a point: as, you have said or done enough, you may stop ''there.''—'''4.''' In that state or condition of things; in that respect.

{{smaller block|To die, to sleep;<br>To sleep: perchance to dream: ay, ''there'''s the rub. {{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, iii. 1. 65.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Mary.'' Of a pure life?<br>''Renard.'' . . . Yea, by Heaven . . . You are happy in him ''there.'' {{right|''Tennyson'', Queen Mary, i. 5.}}}}

'''5.''' Used by way of calling the attention to something, as to a person, object, or place: as, ''there'' is my hand.

{{smaller block|Some wine, within ''there'', and our viands! {{right|''Shak.'', A. and C., iii. 11. 73.}}}}

'''6.''' Used as an indefinite grammatical subject, in place of the real subject, which then follows the verb, increased force being thus secured: so used especially with the verb ''to be:'' as, ''there is'' no peace for the wicked.

{{smaller block|A Knight ''ther was'', and that a worthy man. {{right|''Chaucer'', Gen. Prol. to C. T., l. 43.}}}}

{{smaller block|And God said, Let ''there be'' light; and ''there was'' light. {{right|Gen. i. 3.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
¶
{{smaller block|''There'' appears a new face of things every day.
{{right|''Bacon'', Political Fables, ix., Expl.}}}}

{{smaller block|''There'' seems no evading this conclusion.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Social Statics, p. 433.}}}}

7. Used like ''that'' in interjectional phrases: such as, ''there'''s a darling! ''there'''s a good boy!

{{smaller block|Grandam will
Give it a plum, a cherry, and a fig:
''There'''s a good grandam!
{{right|''Shak.'', K. John, ii. 1. 163.}}}}

{{smaller block|Do your duty,
''There'''s a beauty.
{{right|''W. S. Gilbert'', Fairy Curate.}}}}

8†. Thence.

{{smaller block|For in my paleys, paradys, in persone of an addre,
Falseliche thow fettest ''there'' thynge that I loued.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), xviii. 334.}}}}

{{smaller|'''All there.''' See ''all.''—'''Here and there.''' See ''here''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Here by there†''', here and there. ''Spenser.''—'''Neither here nor there.''' See ''here''<sup>1</sup>.—'''That . . . there''', a colloquial pleonasm intended to emphasize the demonstrative use of ''that'' before its noun: as, ''that'' man ''there.'' In illiterate speech the noun is often transposed after ''there:'' as, ''that there'' boy.—'''To get there''', to succeed in doing something; be successful. [Slang.]}}

'''II.'''† ''conj.'' (''rel. adv.'') Where.

{{smaller block|For I herde onys how Conscience it tolde,
That ''there'' a man were crystened by kynde he shulde be buryed.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), xi. 66.}}}}

{{smaller block|She is honoured over al ''ther'' she goth.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Prol. to Wife of Bath's Tale, l. 237.}}}}

{{smaller block|''There'' corne is, sette hem XV foote atwene,
And XXV there as lande is lene.
{{right|''Palladius'', Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 77.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
{{smaller block|There is a lake of fresh water three myles in compasse, in the midst an Isle containing an acre or ''thereabout.''
{{right|Quoted in ''Capt. John Smith's'' Works, I. 106.}}}}

'''thereabouts''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr′a-bouts″), ''adv.'' [< ''thereabout'' + adv. gen. ''-s.''] Same as ''thereabout'', 2 and 3.

{{smaller block|Some weeke or ''thereabouts.''
{{right|''Heywood'', Fair Maid of the West (Works, ed. 1874, II. 275).}}}}

{{smaller block|She could see the interior of the summer-house. . . . Clifford was not ''thereabouts.''
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Seven Gables, xvi.}}}}

'''thereafter''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr-ȧf′tėr), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''therefter'', ''tharafter'' (= OS. ''tharafter'' = OFries. ''therefter'', ''derefter'' = D. ''daarachter'' = Sw. Dan. ''derefter''); < ''there'' + ''after''.] 1†. After that; after them.

{{smaller block|Wol he have pleynte or teres or I wende?
I have ynogh, if he ''therefter'' sende.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, iv. 861.}}}}

2. After that; afterward.

{{smaller block|And whan thow hast thus don, departe for god, and for thy soule all thy tresour, for thow maiste not longe ''ther-after'' lyven.
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), i. 92.}}}}

{{smaller block|And all at once all round him rose in fire, . . .
And presently ''thereafter'' follow'd calm.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Coming of Arthur.}}}}

3. According to that; after that rule or way; after that sort or fashion; accordingly.

{{smaller block|The fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom; a good understanding have all they that do ''thereafter.''
{{right|''Book of Common Prayer'', Psalter, Ps. cxi. 10.}}}}

{{smaller block|Well perceaving which way the King enclin'd, every one ''thereafter'' shap'd his reply.
{{right|''Milton'', Hist. Eng., iv.}}}}

4†. According.

{{smaller block|''Shal.'' How a score of ewes now?
''Sil. Thereafter'' as they be; a score of good ewes may be worth ten pounds.
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., iii. 2. 56.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|Tell me, if food were now before thee set,<br>Wouldst thou not eat?—''Thereafter'' as I like<br>The giver, answer'd Jesus.{{right|''Milton'', P. R., ii. 321.}}}}

