'''thereagain†''', ''adv.'' [< ME. ''theragayn'', ''theraȝen'', ''theronȝæn''; < ''there'' + ''again''.] Thereagainst.

{{smaller block|Withouten hym we have no myght certeyn,<br>If that hym list to stonden ''theragayn''.{{right|''Chaucer'', Friar's Tale, l. 190.}}}}

'''thereagainst''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr′a̤-genst′), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''theragaines''; < ''there'' + ''against''.] Against it; in opposition to it.

{{smaller block|God teacheth us how fearful a thing it is to wound our conscience and do anything ''thereagainst''.{{right|''J. Bradford'', Letters (Parker Soc., 1853), II. 125.}}}}

{{smaller block|Its ends are passed through the side pieces of the frame and tightened ''thereagainst'' by nuts.{{right|''C. T. Davis'', Bricks and Tiles, p. 229.}}}}

'''thereamong''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr′a̤-mung′), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''theramong''; < ''there'' + ''among''.] Among them.

{{smaller block|Spread the slow smile thro' all her company.<br>Three knights were ''thereamong''; and they too smiled.{{right|''Tennyson'', Pelleas and Ettarre.}}}}

'''thereanent''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr′a̤-nent′), ''adv.'' [< ''there'' + ''anent''.] Concerning that; regarding or respecting that matter. [Scotch.]

'''thereas†''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr-az′), ''conj.'' [< ME. ''thereas'', ''theras''; < ''there'' + ''as''<sup>1</sup>.] Where.

{{smaller block|And ''there as'' I haue doone A-mys,<br>Mercy, Ihesu, I wylle Amende.{{right|''Political Poems'', etc. (ed. Furnivall), p. 188.}}}}

{{smaller block|Whanne he was come ''ther as'' she was,<br>Myrabell came.{{right|''Generydes'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 796.}}}}
